---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { vendors, VERIFIED_LABEL } from '../data/site.js';
const pub = vendors.filter(v => v.pricingPublic).length;
---
<Base
  title="How MedSpaStack collects and verifies pricing"
  description="Our sourcing rules, what we refuse to score, our correction policy, and how affiliate commission is kept out of the research."
  crumbs={["How we verify"]}
>
<p class="ms-eyebrow">Method</p>
<h1 class="ms-display">How we collect this</h1>

<p class="ms-lede ms-measure">
  Most software comparison content in this category is written by vendors about themselves, or
  assembled from directory listings that nobody dates. We do something narrower and, we think, more
  useful: read the vendors' own pages, record what they say, stamp the date, and re-check on a
  schedule.
</p>

<h2 class="ms-h2">Where numbers come from</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>The vendor's own published page, or nothing.</strong> Every price on this site was read
    off the vendor's public pricing page. We do not copy figures from Capterra, G2, Software Advice
    or other comparison sites — those are frequently stale and almost never carry a date.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Every figure is dated.</strong> Pricing in this category changes without announcement.
    An undated price is a guess. Ours currently read {VERIFIED_LABEL}.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Re-checked weekly.</strong> An automated pass re-reads each pricing page and flags
    changes for review before anything is republished.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>"Quote only" is recorded as a finding, not a gap.</strong>
    {vendors.length - pub} of {vendors.length} platforms decline to publish rates. That is a real
    fact about how they sell, and it belongs in your shortlist decision.
  </li>
</ol>

<h2 class="ms-h2">What we refuse to do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Score things we cannot verify.</strong> Several vendors market clinical capability in
    terms we could not confirm from public documentation. We label those unverified rather than
    guessing. An unverified label is information; an invented score is noise.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Publish an overall rating out of ten.</strong> The correct platform depends on whether
    you chart treatments, how many locations you run and how your team is shaped. A single ranked
    list would flatten exactly the variables that decide the answer.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Give clinical, legal or regulatory advice.</strong> What your practice must document,
    who must supervise it, and what your state requires are questions for your medical director and
    your state board. We write about software.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Let commission influence placement.</strong> See below.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="ms-h2">How we classify clinical capability</h2>
<p class="ms-measure">
  We sort platforms into clinical EMR, hybrid, booking/POS, and add-on tools, based on whether the
  vendor documents structured or SOAP-style treatment charting, treatment-linked consent capture,
  before-and-after photo management tied to the patient record, and e-prescribing.
</p>
<p class="ms-measure">
  Intake forms and file attachments are common on booking platforms and are <em>not</em> charting.
  That distinction is the single most consequential one in the category, so we apply it strictly and
  say what evidence we used on every vendor page.
</p>

<h2 class="ms-h2">Corrections</h2>
<p class="ms-measure">
  A wrong price is a defect. If you were quoted something that differs from what we publish, send
  us the detail and we will re-read the source and republish with a new verification date, whether
  the correction flatters the vendor or not.
</p>
<p class="ms-measure">
  We correct errors of fact immediately. We do not remove accurate findings because a vendor
  dislikes them. Where a vendor disputes an editorial classification rather than a fact, we will
  publish their position alongside ours.
</p>

<h2 class="ms-h2">Money</h2>
<p class="ms-measure">
  We earn affiliate commission from some of the platforms listed here. That relationship is
  disclosed on every page that carries a paid link, and the
  <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a> names exactly which vendors pay us and which do not.
</p>
<p class="ms-measure">
  The structural safeguard is that our core asset — the pricing database — is generated from
  vendor documentation, not from an editor's preference. Ordering in tables is by price or
  alphabetical, never by commission. Platforms that pay us nothing, including several
  quote-only enterprise systems, are covered on the same terms as those that do.
</p>
<p class="ms-measure">
  If you ever find a page where the commercial relationship appears to have shaped the conclusion,
  that is a serious failure and we want to hear about it.
</p>

<h2 class="ms-h2">Independence, plainly stated</h2>
<p class="ms-measure">
  MedSpaStack is not owned by, funded by, or employed by any software vendor in this category, and
  no vendor has editorial input. We mention this explicitly because it is not the norm here — the
  most visible "independent" comparison resources in med spa software have direct commercial ties
  to platforms they review, sometimes disclosed only in a founder's job title. Readers deserve to
  know which kind of site they are reading.
</p>
</Base>
